J = {}; C = {}

J['j2'] = {'title': 'Scroll Two · Civil Matters, Middle',
 'head': 'Four chapters. Scroll One looked down at the men below; this one looks up at the ruler and out at the state as a whole. Ruler’s virtue comes in grades — emperor, king, hegemon, strong state; ministers come in six right kinds and six wrong; every temperament carries its own defect; and a state can be diagnosed from its six sorts of ruler, nine airs, four disorders and four dangers. The base text carries no general title for this scroll, and the Siku editors reasoned that “Civil Matters, Middle” must have dropped out. The Qing printing whose opening leaf is the cover of this page has it in full.'}
C['c9'] = {'title': 'The Virtue of a Ruler', 'head':
 'The first chapter of the scroll, and the one that gives the book its scale of government.', 'paras': [
 'The Three Augusts said nothing and their transforming power ran to the four seas, so that the world had no one to thank. The Emperor took his measure from heaven and earth, spoke and gave orders, and the world was at peace; ruler and minister gave the credit to each other and the people did not know why things went well. The King controls men by the Way and makes their hearts submit; he sets a square to guard against decline, and though he has a watchful government and armour ready, he has no use for the blade. The Hegemon controls his gentlemen by leverage, binds them by good faith, and employs them by reward: let the faith fail and the gentlemen fall away, let the reward be spoiled and they will not serve.',
 'So the root of ordering a state is punishment and virtue. Heaven completes the year by the two breaths; men complete good government by the two instruments; and even a sage in office cannot use one alone. <b>Much virtue and little punishment: the Five Emperors. Punishment and virtue in halves: the Three Kings. Much punishment and little virtue: the Five Hegemons. Punishment pure, strong and destroyed: Qin.</b>',
 'The rest of the chapter is comparison, case by case, and the two chief sources for it are Yu Shinan’s Brief Discourse on Emperors and Kings and Ban Gu’s appraisals in the History of the Han. Was Gaozu better than Guangwu, the one open-handed and large, the other exact and minute? Cao Zhi’s verdict is hard on Gaozu: his fame was not pure virtue, his conduct was not pure principle, and after his death a savage woman was let loose on his own blood. Yu Shinan’s is the opposite way about: Gaozu’s ministers were the rarest men of any age and he could use them all; to weigh Guangwu against him is to put a good second beside a first.',
 'Can the Zhou’s Cheng and Kang be set beside the Han’s Wen and Jing, as Ban Gu said? Yu Shinan: Cheng and Kang inherited the traces of Wen and Wu with the Duke of Zhou and the Duke of Shao for ministers, transforming a people already thick with goodness — the wind bending the grass is not even a comparison. Wen of Han came to a country with the wreck of Qin still in it, and nearly put punishments out of use. That was the harder thing. And Emperor Wu, of the great talent and the great designs? “Beside the First Emperor of Qin he comes off better. In arrogance, extravagance and cruelty they are near neighbours. Both had more merit than virtue.” The conclusion the chapter reaches about Wu of Han and the First Emperor is the same sentence: <b>they were rulers who established merit, not rulers who preserved what they had.</b>',
]}
C['c10'] = {'title': 'The Conduct of a Minister', 'head':
 'Six right kinds and six wrong, and then a series of cases in which the classification breaks down.', 'paras': [
 'The <b>six right</b>: the <i>sage minister</i>, who sees the hinge of survival and ruin before the shoot has stirred and forestalls it, so that his lord stands clear in a place of honour; the <i>great minister</i>, who empties his own mind, presses the ruler with ritual and right, and covers his faults; the <i>loyal minister</i>, up early and late to bed, tireless in bringing worthy men forward; the <i>wise minister</i>, who sees where a thing will fail and stops the gap at the source, turning the disaster into a benefit; the <i>upright minister</i>, who keeps to the text of the law, takes no gifts and lives plainly; the <i>blunt minister</i>, who will go against the ruler’s face and tell him his faults when the state is in confusion.',
 'The <b>six wrong</b>: the <i>place-holder</i>, safe in his office and his salary, floating with the age; the <i>flatterer</i>, who says “excellent” to everything and finds out privately what the ruler likes; the <i>traitor</i>, sly within and prim without, who brightens the men he wants advanced and blackens the men he wants dropped; the <i>slanderer</i>, clever enough to dress up a wrong and eloquent enough to carry it, who splits a family from inside; the <i>usurper</i>, who takes the power into his own hands and forges the ruler’s orders; and the <i>minister who ruins a state</i>, who leads the ruler into what is not right and shuts out the light.',
 'Then the hard cases. Was Yang Fu, who spoke his mind to the throne, a loyal minister? Master Yuan says no — a blunt man, not a loyal one: he made his ruler’s faults known to everybody. Chen Qun talked with the ruler all day and never once spoke of his faults, and sent in his memorials in such a way that nobody knew: <i>that</i> was loyalty. Ji Bu served under Xiang Yu, was famous for courage, and afterwards let himself be sold as a slave rather than die. Sima Qian: “How low he brought himself!” — and yet he brought himself low on purpose, knowing what he was worth. Guan Zhong would not die for Prince Jiu: “I am not ashamed of not dying for Prince Jiu; I am ashamed that my authority does not reach the other princes.” The chapter’s point is that the same act has no fixed name: it is read off the office a man holds and the work he has undertaken.',
]}
C['c11'] = {'title': 'A Table of Virtues', 'head':
 'A short chapter that turns “virtue” into a table of correctable biases.', 'paras': [
 'Confucius: by nature men are close to one another; by practice they drift apart. So the sage leads men’s natures, cuts back the drift, is careful whom he lets them keep company with, and trims what is one-sided in them. “Examine what a man likes and hates, order his passions and his nature, and the king’s way is complete.”',
 'The way to order a nature is <b>to find out what you have too much of and strengthen what you are short of</b>. The quick and penetrating should beware of looking too closely; the little-read and little-travelled of being walled in; the bold and hard of being violent; the kind and warm of never deciding; the calm and easy of being late; the large-minded of forgetting.',
 'Then the twelve materials of the Treatise on Personalities, each with its own failure built in. Severe, straight, firm and resolute: his material is in setting things right, his fault is in cutting and exposing. Yielding, easy and forgiving: his beauty is breadth, his fault is that he never decides. Fierce and heroic: his charge is daring, his fault is suspicion. Careful and cautious: his good is in scruple, his fault is doubt. Strong and stubborn: his use is as a main timber, his fault is obstinacy. Subtle in argument: his ability is untying knots, his fault is drifting. Broad and universal: his height is in covering everything, his fault is muddiness. Clean and incorruptible: his distinction is frugality, his fault is narrowness. And so on to the twelfth. “These are the materials of constraint and height; they are not the virtue of the mean.”',
 'And the Wenzi: let the heart be small and the will large, the wit round and the conduct square, the abilities many and the affairs few. A small heart thinks of trouble before it is born; a large will takes in every country and makes itself the hub; round wit has no end and flows to the four quarters; square conduct stands upright and is not bent, and does not change its grip in adversity.',
]}
C['c12'] = {'title': 'Order and Disorder', 'head':
 'The state treated as an object that can be diagnosed. Three tables, and the reading runs backwards: not “is this ruler good?” but “which of these airs does the country smell of?”', 'paras': [
 '<b>The six rulers</b> (Xun Yue’s): the one whose body is upright and whose nature is humane, who acts for others and not for himself, is a <i>king-ruler</i>. The one who masters himself, asks questions and puts things into practice, who acts from what is right and not from his mood, is a <i>governing ruler</i>. The one who works at his business and does not dare be slack, who puts the public first, is a <i>surviving ruler</i>. The one in whom public and private run side by side and who wins one and loses one, is a <i>declining ruler</i>. The one whose feeling outruns his sense of right and whose measures go past the limit, is an <i>endangered ruler</i>. The one who uses slanderers and drives out the loyal, who indulges himself, spends the public money on favours and punishes in anger past the law, and who papers over a wrong rather than mend it, is a <i>doomed ruler</i>.',
 '<b>The nine airs</b>: ruler and minister close and yet ceremonious, the many officers in accord without being identical, yielding and not contending, industrious and not resentful — the air of <i>a state with ritual</i>. Custom not uniform, office not weighty, small officials slandering and commoners getting up opinions — <i>a declining state</i>. Ruler and minister competing in cleverness, the court competing in merit, grandees in reputation, commoners in profit — <i>a state at odds</i>. Many desires above, many tricks below, no settled law and government coming out of several doors — <i>a state in disorder</i>. Extravagance called breadth, arrogance called height, licence called being at ease, while keeping the rites is called being hidebound — <i>a wasted state</i>. Harshness called perception, profit called public spirit, cutting down those below called ability, attaching oneself above called loyalty — <i>a treacherous state</i>. And so to the airs of a state in danger and a state about to fall.',
 'Then the four disorders and the four dangers. The chapter is a diagnostic sheet, and every item on it is something an outsider could observe in a week.',
]}

J['j3'] = {'title': 'Scroll Three · Civil Matters, Lower',
 'head': 'The hinge of the book, and the source of the name it now goes by: the thirteenth chapter, “Reversal,” stands here. The first two scrolls built a whole apparatus of statecraft; this scroll turns the whole of it over. Benevolence, right, ritual, music, name, law, punishment and reward each produce their own opposite; the fourteenth chapter sets the maxims of antiquity against each other in fifty-three pairs and declines to adjudicate; the fifteenth says a law out of step with its time makes the disorder worse the more firmly it is enforced; the sixteenth goes through the schools of thought one by one and gives each its virtue and its disease. A reader who took only this scroll would not have read the book wrongly.'}
C['c13'] = {'title': 'Reversal', 'head':
 'The chapter the modern title comes from. It is not against law and order; it is a warning that every good instrument carries its own way of going bad.', 'paras': [
 'The three dynasties did not fall because their laws fell. <b>The men who handled the laws were not the right men.</b> So law is the footprint the former kings left; without the man, the way does not walk by itself.',
 'Yin Wenzi: benevolence, right, ritual, music, name, law, punishment, reward — these eight were the art by which the Five Emperors and Three Kings governed. And then, one by one, the reverse of each. <b>Benevolence</b> is how favour is spread over things; it is also how partiality is born. <b>Right</b> is how a standard of conduct is set up; it is also how showy falsehood is made. <b>Ritual</b> is how respect and care are practised; it is also how idleness and contempt come in. <b>Music</b> is how feeling and will are harmonised; it is also how licence gets loose. <b>Name</b> is how high and low are set straight; it is also how self-importance and usurpation grow. <b>Law</b> is how a diverse multitude is evened out; it is also how division is made. <b>Punishment</b> is how the unsubmissive are overawed; it is also how brutality is bred. <b>Reward</b> is how loyalty and ability are encouraged; it is also how mean contention starts.',
 'The Wenzi runs the same operation on institutions. Writing was made to manage a hundred affairs, so that the dull should not forget and the clever should keep record; when it declined, men used it to get the guilty off and kill the innocent. Parks were made to supply the ancestral temple and train the soldiers; when they declined, men galloped and hunted in them and stole the people’s season. Raising the worthy was done to even out the teaching and straighten the lawsuits; when it declined, factions formed and each pushed its own.',
 'The Han Shi Waizhuan on the “five reversals” of a gentleman: he has rank and honour and does not use them to love men and act rightly, but turns them into arrogance; he has a rich house and does not use it to relieve want, but turns it into unmeasured display; he has courage and does not use it to guard his lord, but turns it into private brawling; he has wit and does not use it to set up straight designs, but turns it into cunning and fraud; he has good looks and does not use them to preside over a court, but turns them into seduction. And the Duke of Tai, which is the coldest formulation in the chapter: “Make the punishments plain and men are cowed; cow them and something breaks out. Look too closely and men are harassed; harass them and they move; move them and they are not settled, and it is easy for a change to come.”',
]}
C['c14'] = {'title': 'Right and Wrong', 'head':
 'The most peculiar chapter in the book in form. Fifty-three pairs, each headed “the case for” and “the case against,” each made only of quotations, and the author scarcely intervenes: his own notes in this chapter come to barely three hundred characters.', 'paras': [
 'It opens by saying why: gain and loss take different roads, plain and ornamented make different governments; the records of the past hand down their teaching, and every one of them can now be argued against with reason.',
 '<b>For:</b> the Greater Odes — “clear-sighted and wise, and so he keeps himself safe”; the Changes — “the great virtue of heaven and earth is life.” <b>Against:</b> the Analects — “a gentleman faced with danger gives up his life”; and again, “a gentleman will kill himself to complete his benevolence; he will not seek life at the cost of it.”',
 '<b>For:</b> Guanzi — “doubt the present, look at the past; not knowing what is to come, look at what has gone.” <b>Against:</b> the Lüshi Chunqiu — “a man choked to death on food, and you would forbid the world to eat: absurd. A man drowned from a boat, and you would forbid the world to sail: absurd. A man lost his state by using arms, and you would abolish the world’s arms: absurd.” Du Shu: “Traitors have existed from antiquity to now. One in a century is treading on the last one’s heels; one in a thousand li is shoulder to shoulder. To take them as a warning is to stop men eating because of one who choked: the choked are few and the starved will be many.”',
 '<b>For:</b> Confucius — “I hate those who take exposing evil for uprightness.” <b>Against:</b> Guanzi — “I hate those who take concealing evil for benevolence.” And Cao Xi’s Discourse on Impartiality: covering up the bad and displaying the good is what the lanes and alleys say; it was invented to stop men slandering each other out of love and hate, and it is not an exact principle nor a fair judgement. Where good and bad are not told apart, disorder comes of it; where friends are all of one voice, ruin follows.',
 'And so, pair by pair, through fifty-three of them. The reader is left holding the scales himself, which is the point. <i>(On this page the two headings are set as labels, because in the Siku printing they stand in the small type used for notes and are not part of the sentences they head.)</i>',
]}
C['c15'] = {'title': 'Fitting the Change', 'head':
 'On time. This is the chapter whose second half was missing from the punctuated base text and has been restored here from the Siku edition — some two thousand seven hundred characters, containing the whole assessment of the Huang-Lao, Confucian, Mohist and Legalist methods.', 'paras': [
 'The former kings made their measures to fit their time and shaped affairs as the work required; the law suited its time and so there was order, the business fitted its task and so there was success. Now the time has moved and the law has not; the task has changed and men still do it after the ancients. <b>So the law and the time contradict each other, and the more firmly the law is set up the worse the disorder grows.</b>',
 'A king’s government first removes what harms men and gives them food and clothing, then teaches them ritual and right, then overawes them with punishment so that they know what to like and what to avoid. That is the king’s art. The hegemon’s art is to honour the ruler and lower the minister, to keep authority in one place, to let no order come from a second door, to keep faith with reward and punishment, and to see that the officers are in order and the orders are carried out.',
 'Then the Laozi’s “I do nothing and the people transform themselves,” with the Wenzi’s gloss, which is the best short definition of non-action in Chinese: “Non-action does not mean that you cannot be drawn and cannot be pushed. It means acting along the grain of things, establishing merit out of what is already there, pushing what is naturally so.” Tang and Wu were sage rulers and could not have taken a skiff on the lakes with the men of Yue; Yi Yin was a worthy minister and could not have ridden with the Hu; Confucius and Mozi were universally learned and could not have gone through the thickets and the crags with men who live in the hills.',
 'And then the four methods, each with its examples and each given its due. <b>Huang-Lao</b>: Cao Can governed Qi by it and the state settled; handing over to his successor he said only, “I leave you the prisons and the markets — mind you do not disturb them much,” and when asked whether there was nothing more important, answered, “The prison and the market are where things are allowed to be contained together. Disturb them and where are the bad men to go?” Qin used extreme punishment and the empire rebelled; Emperor Wu used sharp law and the jails filled. “That is the harm it does.” <b>The Confucian method</b>: the seven teachings and the three utmosts, from the Kongzi Jiayu — honour the old and those below grow more filial; be glad to give and those below grow more open; love virtue and nothing is hidden from you; hate greed and those below are ashamed to contend. <b>The Mohist</b>: the chapters on moderating palaces and clothes, quoted nearly whole. <b>The Legalist</b>: Shang Yang, Shen Buhai, Shenzi, Yin Wenzi, Han Fei and Li Si on names and shares — “when the name and the share are undetermined, even Yao and Shun and Yu and Tang would all join the chase; once determined, even a poor thief does not dare take.”',
 'The chapter will not choose. <b>“Different as their warp and weft may be, and different the failure each repairs, in bringing the multitude through alive they all have something in them.”</b> What it will not have is the man who quotes a rule made for one age to refute a remedy made for another, or the man who cites the style of the ancient kings to sneer at a hegemon’s government without asking what time it is. “He speaks falsely and argues well, he follows the wrong and makes it glossy: this man is a criminal, and the gentleman forbids him.”',
]}
C['c16'] = {'title': 'Setting the Schools Straight', 'head':
 'A survey of the nine currents of thought, modelled on the bibliographical treatise of the History of the Han but with a paragraph added to each school beginning “and when it goes wrong.”', 'paras': [
 'The standard is set first, from the Record of Rites. The Odes teach warmth and depth, the Documents breadth of reference, Music breadth and ease, the Changes cleanness and fineness, Ritual respect and restraint, the Annals the ordering of words and the arrangement of events. And each has its failure: the Odes make men simple, the Documents make them false, Music makes them extravagant, the Changes make them injurious, Ritual makes them fussy, the Annals make them unruly. A man deep in the Odes is warm without being simple; deep in the Documents, wide without being false — and so through the six.',
 '<b>The Confucians</b> came out of the office of instruction: they help the ruler follow the two breaths and make the teaching clear, they range in the six classics and attend to benevolence and right. That is their height. Where it goes wrong, the confused lose the fineness and the cranks trim to the fashion, until men leave the root of the way and make a noise to catch a crowd. <b>The Daoists</b> came out of the office of the scribes, who kept the record of success and failure; they hold the essential, keep the root, guard themselves by emptiness and hold themselves by lowliness — the art of the man who faces south. That is their height. Where it goes wrong, they would abolish ritual and music and throw out benevolence and right and govern by emptiness alone, which cannot be done.',
 '<b>The Yin-Yang school</b> came out of the astronomical office and reverently follows heaven, which is their height; where it goes wrong they are bound by taboos and swallowed by the small arts. <b>The Legalists</b> came out of the office of law: reward and punishment are made to accord with the ritual order, which is their height; where it goes wrong they are pitiless and cut away the teaching and the transformation. <b>The Logicians</b> came from the ritual officers and are good at rectifying names; where it goes wrong they tangle and cut and turn it into a game. <b>The Mohists</b> came from the temple guardians: they honour thrift and the worthy, and where it goes wrong they see no distinction of rank. <b>The Diplomats</b> came from the office of envoys and are good at responding to a situation; where it goes wrong they make deceit their whole trade. <b>The Miscellanists</b> and <b>the Agriculturalists</b> follow, each in the same form.',
]}

